  "text": "If an influencer comes up to you in the street and says For $5, how many states are there in the US? Don't instantly answer them, because you could be engaging in an illegal act of gambling. According to the US lottery laws, for a game to be an illegal lottery, it has to have elements of prize, chance, and consideration. So if you ever use the coin flip to decide whether you or your friend pays for dinner, that technically could have been unlicensed gambling, since there's a prize, dinner, determined by chance, a coin flip, and you're risking paying for the entire bill, consideration, Or worse, if you're American and have ever had a group dinner, you may have committed illegal gambling by playing credit card roulette. It's where you let the waiter randomly pick a credit card that would pay for yours and everyone's dinner with the risk of your card being selected. Of course, no one will arrest you for engaging in social gambling like this. And an influencer offering you $5 to answer a question is likely just a giveaway, since it lacks the element of you putting your money on the line. But let's say you answer his question correctly and he offers you this. Do you want to go double or nothing? Name five countries other than the US. Now you're actually betting the $5 that you previously won, which is the final piece needed for it to count as gambling or an illegal lottery. Or at least that's what I thought. Because it turns out that trivia questions like these are not seen as chance, but rather a skill-based outcome. So unless they start asking you truly random stuff, like what number am I thinking of? You're good. It's not illegal gambling. But to be honest, I think you have a better chance of guessing the number in their head than naming five countries other than the US. Um, London? Mm-mm."
